{"name":"Acacia Green","hex":"#486241","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"acacia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub or tree of the tribe Acacieae. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The thickened or dried juice of several species in Acacieae, in particular Vachellia nilotica (syn. Acacia nilotica, Egyptian acacia)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A roll or bag, filled with dust, borne by Byzantine emperors, as a memento of mortality. It is represented on medals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/acacia"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Acacia","blocks":[{"text":"Acacia, commonly known as wattles or acacias, is a genus of about 1,084 species of shrubs and trees in the subfamily Mimosoideae of the pea family Fabaceae. Initially, it comprised a group of plant species native to Africa, South America, and Australasia, but is now reserved for species mainly from Australia, with others from New Guinea, Southeast Asia, and the Indian Ocean."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG/330px-Acacia_baileyana_3%2C_Canberra_ACT.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acacia"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg/500px-Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg/1280px-Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg/500px-Acacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bipinnate leaves of Acacia dealbata","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcacia_dealbata_kz09.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Acacia_penninervis_%285368395701%29.jpg/500px-Acacia_penninervis_%285368395701%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Acacia_penninervis_%285368395701%29.jpg/1280px-Acacia_penninervis_%285368395701%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&u